{:tier :glm-5.2, :verse 23, :comments [{:quotes [{:he "השמש יצא וגו'", :translit "ha-shemes yatza vegomer", :gloss "The sun came forth, etc."} {:he "הפסוק מתחיל בה\"א ומסיים בה\"א", :translit "ha-pasuk matchil be-heh u-mesayem be-heh", :gloss "The verse begins with heh and ends with heh"} {:he "מלמד שהלך ה' מילין קודם שיצא השמש", :translit "melamed she-halach HaShem milin kodem she-yatza ha-shemesh", :gloss "teaching that HaShem walked milin before the sun came forth"}], :mechanisms [{:kind :other, :note "Verse begins and ends with the letter heh, signaling that God journeyed milin before sunrise"}], :frame {:text "The verse Genesis 19:23 begins with ה (השמש) and ends with ה (וגו' — the verse's final word בואך ends in heh, or the phrase is understood as enclosed by the letter heh). The repetition of heh alludes to ה, the divine name, indicating that God Himself accompanied the journey. The Gemara states that Resh Lakish said: one who walks a mil on the road before sunrise is saved from harmful forces (Pesachim 2a). The Aramaic Targum of Genesis 19:23–24 renders that Lot entered Zoar as the sun rose and God destroyed Sodom, showing divine presence active at the precise moment of dawn.", :sources ["Genesis 19:23" "Pesachim 2a" "Targum Onkelos Genesis 19:23-24"]}}], :arithmetic [{:kind :other, :note "Verse begins and ends with the letter heh, signaling that God journeyed milin before sunrise", :by :selah, :ran? false}], :book :genesis, :voice :baal-haturim, :lang :en, :chapter 19, :discipline "bht-chalkboard-v1", :timestamp "2026-07-13T00:32:57.614598862Z", :model "glm-5.2"}